":495},"avgTime":{"current":"10:20:24","previous":"10:18:26","deltaSeconds":118}}},"historicalRank":{"fieldSizeRank":4,"finishersRank":4,"finishRateRank":89,"winningTimeRank":12,"winningTimeRankDir":8,"totalRaces":98},"recordContext":{"womenWouldHaveWonMen":{"mostRecentYear":1966,"mensWinner":"Tommy MALONE","mensTime":"06:14:07"}},"narrative":"The 2018 Comrades Marathon was the 93rd running of the race, run in the 'down' direction from Pietermaritzburg to Durban over 90.184 kms. The race was held on Sunday, 10 June. Conditions: Cold at start. Bright early morning sunshine, warming to mild, becoming overcast at midday. Muted sunshine late afternoon. Runners had 12 hours to complete the course.\n\nBongmusa MTHEMBU claimed the men's title in 05:26:34, ahead of Joseph MPHUTHI (05:35:09) and Steven WAY (05:35:27). Ann ASHWORTH won the women's race in 06:10:04, followed by Gerda STEYN (06:15:34).\n\nAnn ASHWORTH's 06:10:04 was faster than the men's winning time as recently as 1966 (Tommy MALONE, 06:14:07).\n\nFrom a field of 23,423 entries, 16,484 runners crossed the finish line (70.4% completion rate), with athletes representing over 19 countries. The field comprised 13,108 men and 3,376 women (20.5% female). The average finish time was 10:20:25.\n\nMedals awarded: 20 Gold, 10 Wally Hayward, 588 Silver, 2,495 Bill Rowan, 7,454 Bronze, 5,917 Vic Clapham. 4,431 runners (26.9%) were completing their first Comrades. The final finisher was Lunga ROSHE in 12:00:00.\n\nThis edition featured the 4th largest field in race history and the 4th most finishers ever. Compared to the previous down run in 2016, the field was 8.7% larger.\n\nVeterans and masters athletes made up 57% of the finishers, with 7,083 seniors (avg 10:11:03) and 9,400 in the veteran categories.","countryDataPct":100.0}